		<h2 class="title text-xl">Research</h2>

		<h3>CT Radiomics for Pancreatic Tumor Treatment Analysis</h3>
		<p><em>Thayer School of Engineering, Dartmouth College — with Mayo Clinic</em></p>
		<p>December 2025 – June 2026</p>
		<p>
			I worked on building a machine learning pipeline to help predict whether pancreatic cancer
			patients respond to photodynamic therapy (PDT) using just their CT scans. There are subtle
			texture changes in CT images that the human eye can't pick up, so then our group considered
			whether a model could. I extracted radiomic features from longitudinal scans, ranked them
			using mRMR selection, and tested their stability through bootstrapping. From there, I used PCA
			to reduce dimensionality and trained an SVM classifier to separate responders from
			non-responders. The top features ended up being GLCM Entropy and Shape Compactness, which our
			collaborating radiologists confirmed made clinical sense. We also saw morphological tumor
			volume decrease across timepoints, which was encouraging yet not a full indicator of tumor
			deletion. I have attached a poster of my work, which I presented at <a
				href="https://students.dartmouth.edu/surfd/news-events/wetterhahn-science-symposium/2026-symposium"
				target="_blank"
				rel="noopener noreferrer">Dartmouth's Wetterhahn Symposium</a
			>.
		</p>
		<p>
			<a
				href="/files/research/Andrew Hwang - POGUE Poster.pdf"
				target="_blank"
				rel="noopener noreferrer">View Poster (PDF)</a
			>
		</p>
		<p>
			<strong>Tools:</strong> Python, Scikit-Learn, LIFEx, 3D Slicer<br />
			<strong>Methods:</strong> Radiomics, mRMR, PCA, SVM (RBF kernel), bootstrap stability analysis<br
